ough D_i and possibly other points. Since each line can intersect each column at most one point, and each column has at most one point from each line. So we need to assign points from columns 1,2,3 to lines such that each line gets at most one point per column.\n\nLet's list points by column:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) (the last is D1)\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) (the last is D2)\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2) (the last is D3)\nColumn 4: (4,1) (D4)\n\nWe need to assign each point to a line. D1 is assigned to L1, D2 to L2, D3 to L3, D4 to L4.\n\nNow we need to assign the remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3) in column 1; (2,1),(2,2) in column 2; (3,1) in column 3. That's 6 points.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each can take at most one point from each column. So column 1 has 3 points to assign to 4 lines (excluding L1 which already has D1 in column 1). So we can assign (1,1) to L2, (1,2) to L3, (1,3) to L4, for example. Column 2 has 2 points to assign to 4 lines (excluding L2 which already has D2). So assign (2,1) to L1, (2,2) to L3 (or L4). Column 3 has 1 point (3,1) to assign to 4 lines (excluding L3 which already has D3). So assign (3,1) to L2 maybe.\n\nNow we need to check collinearity: For each line L_i, we have two points (maybe more) assigned. Let's see:\n\n- L1: D1 = (1,4) and (2,1). Are they collinear? Slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, which is sunny. So L1 passes through (1,4) and (2,1). At x=3, y = 4 + (-3)*(3-1) = 4 - 6 = -2, not needed. So L1 covers D1 and (2,1). Good.\n\n- L2: D2 = (2,3) and (1,1) and (3,1). Actually we assigned (1,1) to L2 and (3,1) to L2. That's three points. Are they collinear? Let's check: Points (1,1), (2,3), (3,1). Compute slopes: (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, slope between (1,1) and (2,3) is 2. Slope between (2,3) and (3,1) = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2. Not equal, so they are not collinear. So this assignment fails.\n\nWe need to assign points such that each line's assigned points are collinear. So we need to find a partition of the points into 4 collinear subsets, eac